    is the symbolisation target that the Rust std backtrace `LoadLibrary`s at
    panic time, so it exists only as a string in the binary. An earlier
    extraction with `strings -a <file> | grep -oiE '[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+\.dll'`
    miscounted it as an 8th entry: `strings` is a heuristic that can only
    over-include, and cannot tell load-time imports, delay imports and
    incidental strings apart. **The conclusion is unchanged — the two match
    item for item; what changed is the method and the number (8 -> 7).** If
    objdump is not at hand and the `strings` method is used anyway, the
    crate's own module name must be excluded as well as `dbghelp.dll` — it is
    `audio_capture.dll` in the self-built artifact and
    `audio_capture_napi.dll` in the original (both are the build output's name
    before it was renamed to `.node`, not an external dependency; the two
    names differ, so both must be excluded). **This is stronger evidence than
    the Linux comparison above**, where the self-built artifact added two
    libraries; and with the import table the two methods are now on equal
    footing as well (`readelf -d`'s `NEEDED` and the PE import table are both
    authoritative readings, not string scans).
